        'Phishing is a fake message, by email, text, Teams or phone, that tries to make you click a link, open an attachment or give your password. It is the most common way in, because it is cheap.\n\nSigns: urgency (“today”), something you do not expect, a sender that is almost right (steel-brouwer.co instead of .com), and a link that points to a different site than the text says. Hover over the link without clicking.',
        'Spear phishing is phishing aimed at one person or role, using knowledge the attacker gathered first, for example on LinkedIn: “buyer at a machine builder”. Such an email looks like a real invoice from a real supplier.\n\nSocial engineering is the wider term: manipulating people instead of cracking technology. A call “from the helpdesk” asking for your code, or someone in a hi-vis vest who follows you through the gate (tailgating).',
